course: dictionary
lesson: 36
title: One word, many meanings: academy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. You can often work out which one a writer means by reading the words around it. «Only a tiny share of boys who join a football academy young become professionals.» Here the clues are join, young and become professionals, so an academy is a place where young people are trained in a special skill.
explain: Now read this one: «The Grammy Awards are voted on by members of the Recording Academy, people who work in music, not by fans.» The words people who work in music are the clue. Here an academy is a society of experts, a group of people who share a skill and help decide things about it.
explain: How do you know which meaning fits? Try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense. A training place fits the football sentence. A society of experts fits the Grammy sentence. A capital letter in a name, like Royal Academy or Plato's Academy, is another clue that the word names a particular place or group.
fact: An academy is a school or college, often one that trains people in a special skill, such as music, dance, football or the police.
fact: In England, an academy is a kind of state school paid for by the government but run by a trust instead of the local council.
fact: An academy can also be a society of famous experts, such as a national academy of sciences.
fact: In ancient Greece, the Academy was the school of the philosopher Plato.
fact: Words like «academies» can have several meanings, so always check the clues in the sentence.
exercise: «Only a tiny share of boys who join a football academy young become professionals.» What is a football academy in this sentence? | *A place where young players are trained | A kind of shop that sells boots | A team of famous experts | A ball game played on grass
exercise: The Recording Academy is a group of people who work in music. What kind of group is it? | *A society of experts | A type of football team | A kind of shop that sells instruments | A school for very young children
exercise: Plato's Academy was a famous school in ancient Greece. What was it? | A society of artists in Italy | *A school of the philosopher Plato | A type of painting | A football club
test:
«For centuries women were often kept out of art academies; some, like Artemisia Gentileschi in 1600s Italy, still became famous.» What are art academies here? | A kind of paint | *Schools or societies where artists are trained | Shops that sell paintings | Museums with no paintings in them
In England, what is an academy school? | A private school where parents pay the fees | A school run by the local council | *A state school paid for by the government but run by a trust | A school for adults only
exam:
«Some academies now group young players by maturity rather than only age.» What does academies mean in this sentence? | A kind of fish | A famous painter | *Places that train young players | A type of school exam
